1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,900
Take one function and write it four times:
Python, Java, C, and Vinglish.

2
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:15,000
The braces change. The types move. But the behavior is the same:
keep, transform, combine.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Vinglish is a language I built.
Zero is deliberately separate from it.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
The compiler exports versioned JSON;
Zero sees only the meaning on the other side.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:40,200
Programming languages express syntax differently;
they express intent similarly.

6
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:52,000
Each adapter lowers a language through its own frontend
into a shared Semantic Graph.

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:02,600
It is deliberately not an AI guess.
Facts become evidence; deterministic constraints evaluate hypotheses.

8
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:18,000
This function becomes an ordered pipeline:
Filter, Mapper, Reducer.

9
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:31,600
The compiler says only “type mismatch.”
Zero sees an accumulator and the semantic role that broke.

10
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:44,000
Its fixes are tied to that role.

11
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:55,600
Same graph. Same evidence. Same answer.
The report is byte-stable.

12
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:06,000
32 semantic patterns are verified across Python, Java, C,
and Vinglish transport.

13
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:19,400
I used Codex with GPT-5.6 as an engineering partner:
Rust crates, adapters, corpus, verification, and launch tooling.

14
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:31,000
The final conclusion stays inspectable code,
not a model response.

15
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Ask for a meaning: filter then reduce.
The query matches stored semantic reports, not filenames.

16
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Vinglish Zero. Different syntax. Same meaning.
Every conclusion comes with its trail.
